  "sources": [
    "style-research-extended vol.4 jersey-club — \"Two neighbouring archetypes are recommended and do not exist in this app: `baltimore-club` and `philly-club`… 2Rare + Lay Bankz + DJ Diamond Kuts to `philly-club`\"",
    "style-research-extended vol.4 jersey-club — tempo: use 135–140 for Jersey/Philly-facing material; club-rap vocal records sit at 132–138",
    "style-research-extended vol.4 jersey-club — the backbeat is on 2 & 4 and there is no half-time reading of this lane; a generator that half-times it produces drill, not club",
    "style-research-extended vol.4 jersey-club — harmony is near-absent, inherited whole from the sampled source, and \"bright and consonant is normal — Jersey is a happy idiom\"",
    "style-research-extended-4 vol.4 header — the divergences specified: 130 flat, 8/4 two-bar unit, softer kick, horn stabs"
  ],
  "notes": "The club lane's **rap-vocal wing** — the one a producer reaches for when the record is a song rather than a DJ tool. ⛔ **It is authored between its two cousins on purpose, and the tempo is the first half of that.** The research gives one band per city: Baltimore 130–134, Jersey/Philly-facing 135–140, and *\"club-rap vocal records sit at 132–138\"* — which is this lane exactly, so 132–140 centred on 136, above `baltimore-club`'s flat 130 and below `jersey-club`'s 138-centred 130–145 top end. ⛔ **The second half is the kick, and it is softer than Jersey's by the same sourced sentence** that separates Baltimore from Jersey: Jersey's divergence is *\"harder kicks and chop the samples up a lot more\"*. The five-kick bar belongs to Jersey and is pinned by a gate; this lane sits under it at four to five, less syncopated, with the 808 following rather than leading. ⛔ **What it has that neither cousin does is a topline.** These are vocal rap records, so the melodic part is song-shaped rather than a chopped fragment: `structure` is `call_response` like the family, but `densityPerBar` is the highest of the three, `emptyBarBias` the lowest, and the harmony is allowed to move — three and four chord families, and **major-leaning**, because the research says of the whole lane that *\"bright and consonant is normal — Jersey is a happy idiom, which is the affective difference from drill\"*. ⚠ **Still not a progression engine.** The family rule — *\"do not generate progressions here\"* — is honoured by keeping `maxChords` at 3 and weighting the vamp heavily; a club-rap record that does carry a progression got it from an R&B or pop sample, and a sample is not a generated chord track. ⚠ **No half-time reading, ever.** *\"A generator that half-times this lane produces drill, not club.\"* `halfTime` is false and there is no mode that flips it — which is the one thing `michigan-offgrid`, authored the same day, deliberately does do. ⚠ **The kick-lane-only swing is not expressible**, the same as `baltimore-club`: the source asks for 0% globally with 8–16% on the kick's cluster alone, and this engine swings a grid rather than a lane, so it is left straight and the shuffle is carried by `tresilloBias` and the anchors. ⚠ **Nothing was moved onto this model.** 2Rare, Lay Bankz and DJ Diamond Kuts are named by the research as the roster that should move here from `jersey-club`, and `jersey-club` still carries its own `club-rap` mode. Both are migrations that saved projects reproduce from, and they belong in their own change. ⚠ **INFERRED, hence `medium`.** Sourced: the tempo band, the softer kick, the 2-and-4 backbeat, the no-half-time rule, the near-absent harmony and the bright affect. Inferred: every velocity tier, every register, the key and scale lists, the `intervals`/`contour` split and the mode names.",
